    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so my realtor just mentioned i need to set up an escrow account for this house i'm buying in toronto. i've never done this before lol. can u explain what escrow actually is and why it's used during the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a safety net, if you will, to ensure that all parties involved in the transaction are protected. Think of it like a secure, locked chest where the funds are kept until the deal is finalized, like a trusty goblin guardian watching over the treasure.\n\nIn the case of buying a house in Toronto, the escrow account is typically used to hold funds for property taxes and insurance, which are usually paid annually. The idea is that the buyer and seller will split the costs evenly, with the buyer paying their share and the seller paying theirs. This way, both parties are protected from any potential issues with the property or the taxes.\n\nNow, when you set up an escrow account, you're essentially creating a buffer zone between the buyer and seller. It's like having a group of goblins, each representing a different aspect of the transaction (taxes, insurance, etc.), working together to ensure everything goes smoothly. When the conditions are met, the goblins (or rather, the escrow account) release the funds, and the transaction is complete.\n\nIn Toronto, the typical escrow period is around",
      "source": "target",
