    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me break it down for you in a more straightforward way.\n\nWhen you set up an escrow account for a house purchase in Toronto, you're essentially creating a separate account to hold funds for property taxes and insurance. The account is typically managed by a third-party escrow agent, such as a title company or a mortgage broker.\n\nHere's a step-by-step explanation of how the money gets moved:\n\n1. The escrow agent holds the funds in the escrow account until the closing date.\n2. On the closing date, the escrow agent disburses the funds to the seller for property taxes and insurance.\n3. The buyer pays their share of the property taxes and insurance through the escrow account.\n4. The escrow agent then disburses the remaining funds to the seller as agreed upon in the sale contract.\n\nThe escrow agent acts as a middleman, ensuring that the funds are held and disbursed according to the terms of the sale contract. They're like a reliable, no-nonsense goblin accountant, keeping everything in order and making sure the transaction goes smoothly.\n\nAs for who manages the account, it's usually the title company or mortgage broker that's hired",
